    "https://brocku.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/brocku_careers/job/St-Catharines-Main-Campus/Mental-Health-Nurse_JR-1025338": {
      "title": "Mental Health Nurse",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-22\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nBrock University is located on the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples, many of whom continue to live and work here today. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and is within the land protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement.  We are one of Canada’s outstanding comprehensive universities, where excellence and innovation thrive! Brock has been proudly recognized as a Hamilton-Niagara Top Employer for eight consecutive years. Brock continues to earn recognition as one of Canada’s Best Employers and one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity in 2026. For the second consecutive year, Forbes, in partnership with Statista, also named Brock one of Canada’s Top Employers for Company Culture. At Brock, you will find a welcoming, inclusive community and an exciting range of meaningful career opportunities. Ignite new possibilities for your career. Break through at Brock. Post End Date: October 6, 2026 at 11:59 PM This job advertisement is to fill an existing vacancy in the Administrative Professional (Employee Group) About the Role:  Reporting to the Manager, Health and Clinical Operations the Mental Health Nurse provides care and support to students with mental health concerns. This includes triaging and supporting students in crisis, providing clinical mental health assessments, implementing and evaluating care plans. The Mental Health Nurse will also support the Student Health Services medical clinic as needed. The Mental Health Nurse will : Conduct front-line assessments to identify students’ presenting needs, level of risk, and urgency of care, and determine priority access to Student Health Services (SHS) resources in accordance with applicable legislation, professional standards, SHS policies, and organizational objectives; Conduct initial medical assessments to identify physical health conditions that may contribute to, complicate, or impact a student’s mental health presentation; Conduct comprehensive mental health assessments and provide relevant clinical information for review and follow-up by physicians and psychiatrists; Provide timely and compassionate crisis intervention and risk management for students experiencing complex and highly sensitive mental health and wellbeing concerns, including suicidal ideation, depression, abuse, sexual violence, eating disorders, and substance use; Serve as a primary point of contact for students experiencing mental health crises, conducting appropriate risk assessments, implementing interventions, coordinating supports, and facilitating referrals to relevant services and resources; Provide case management support for students by coordinating communication, facilitating access to appropriate services, and supporting collaboration with campus and community resources; Collaborate with psychiatrists, physicians, psychologists, counsellors, and appropriate campus and community partners to facilitate treatment and continuity of care, sharing care plans and relevant health information in accordance with applicable privacy and health information legislation and organizational policies; Liaise with Student Health Services physicians and relevant campus and community resources to support discharge planning following hospitalization, facilitate communication regarding clinical progress, develop transition strategies, and promote continuity of care; Coordinate access to appropriate accommodations, emergency supports, and community resources, including assistance with emergency funding or temporary housing when a student’s circumstances create an immediate risk to their safety or wellbeing; Maintain comprehensive, accurate, and confidential health records in accordance with applicable privacy legislation, including the Personal Health Information Protection Act (PHIPA), and university policies and procedures; Participate in multidisciplinary consultation and case review meetings with physicians, nurses, psychiatrists, and other healthcare professionals to discuss patient history, clinical needs, treatment options, risk management, and outcomes; Act as a liaison between healthcare providers and students, providing clear information, education, and instructions regarding treatment plans, follow-up care, referrals, and available supports; Coordinate and support students’ return to academic and campus life following a mental health crisis, hospitalization, or period of absence, including facilitating communication with appropriate supports and healthcare providers and ensuring relevant documentation, recommendations, and expectations are in place; and Provide backup support to Student Health Services floor nurses, assisting with clinical needs and supporting General Practitioners (GPs) in the assessment, coordination, and delivery of student care as required. Key Skills and Experience : Bachelor's degree in Nursing with Mental Health Certification and/or equivalent combination of education and experience;  Current registration as a RN and in good standing with the College of Nurses of Ontario (CNO); Annual CPR certification; Recent related mental health experience with community mental health experience preferred; Knowledge of, and sensitivity to, the diverse cultural and co-morbid health issues common to our patient demographic; Familiarity with the Mental Health Act and its implications for both patients and providers; Proficiency in clinical nursing interventions and procedures within the scope of practice of a registered Nurse and mental health appropriate to our care setting; Experience triaging psychiatric and mental health disorders, and crisis/trauma counselling; Knowledge and proficiency in general nursing procedures and skills, interventions and management practices particular to the needs of our patients including, knowledge of commonly prescribed medications; Excellent problem solving and critical thinking techniques, leadership and resource management; Excellent organizational skills to prioritize workload in a fast-paced environment with changing deadlines, while maintaining focus on quality service; and The ability to work effectively in an environment with sensitive and confidential information and situations. Preferred or Asset Skills:  Completion of an advanced diploma or certification in psychiatric nursing or equivalent combination of training and experience an asset Salary and Total Rewards:  Job Grade M, Salary Scale ($74,235- $118,776) Target Hiring Range: $74,235  -  $78,000* This is a permanent position that includes:   Health & Dental Benefits: Comprehensive extended health, dental, and vision coverage. Pension Plan: Enrollment eligibility in the Brock University Pension Plan. Vacation: Up to 3 weeks per year, in addition to university holidays. Professional Development: Eligibility for the Brock Tuition Waiver Program. *The final salary is based on experience, internal equity, and budget considerations within the target hiring range. We are committed to offering flexible work arrangements where possible as outlined in our Flexible Work Arrangement Policy . As a student-centered organization, all employees are required to work on campus as needed.   Brock University is committed to creating a respectful and equitable workplace. We strive to foster a culture of diversity and inclusion in our work and learning environments. We welcome applications from all qualified individuals and actively encourage applications from women, people with disabilities, members of the 2SLGBTQIA+ community, Indigenous Peoples, people who identify as Black, African and/or Caribbean, as racialized and/or as from ethnic and cultural minority groups, and other underrepresented demographic groups at Brock and in the Niagara region. Brock also recognizes intersectionality and the interconnected identities, histories, and experiences of these aforementioned groups .   We are committed to inclusive and barrier-free recruitment, and we accommodat e the needs of applicants throughout all stages of the recruitment process, as outlined in our Employment Accommodation Policy and consistent with the requirements of the Ontario Human Rights Code . Please contact talent@brocku.ca   if you require a disability related accommodation so we can ensure you r participation needs are met.   Brock University does not use AI Technology at any stage of the recruitment process.   We appreciate all applications received . C andidates that are selected for an interview will be contacted.   Learn more about Brock University by visiting www.brocku.ca ."
    },
    "https://brocku.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/brocku_careers/job/St-Catharines-Main-Campus/Co-op-Transition-Advisor_JR-1025372": {
      "title": "Co-op Transition Advisor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-22\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nBrock University is located on the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee and Anishinaabe peoples, many of whom continue to live and work here today. This territory is covered by the Upper Canada Treaties and is within the land protected by the Dish with One Spoon Wampum Agreement.  We are one of Canada’s outstanding comprehensive universities, where excellence and innovation thrive! Brock has been proudly recognized as a Hamilton-Niagara Top Employer for eight consecutive years. Brock continues to earn recognition as one of Canada’s Best Employers and one of Canada’s Best Employers for Diversity in 2026. For the second consecutive year, Forbes, in partnership with Statista, also named Brock one of Canada’s Top Employers for Company Culture. At Brock, you will find a welcoming, inclusive community and an exciting range of meaningful career opportunities. Ignite new possibilities for your career. Break through at Brock. Post End Date: October 6, 2026 at 11:59 PM This job advertisement is to fill an existing vacancy in the Administrative Professional (Employee Group) Please note: This is a fixed-term contract beginning by early November 2026, expected to conclude November 2027. About the Role : The Co-op Transition Advisor plays a pivotal role in enhancing student success by helping develop and implementing comprehensive strategies to improve co-op students’ retention and engagement at both Undergraduate and Graduate levels. The Advisor is responsible for collaborating with various University academic and administrative departments and services to create a supportive and inclusive environment that encourage student progression and success through their co-op journey.   The Co-op Transition Advisor will:  Manage the year one (1) internal co-op replenishment strategy including promoting to eligible students, developing and updating admission rubric, assessing eligibility and onboarding new admits for a seamless transition into co-op;   Provide individualized support to co-op students to ensure they are on track with both their co-op and academic progression and sequence;    Conduct regular case conferencing with SWAC to review co-op students with special needs and/or accommodations needs;  Foster partnerships with on campus partners (i.e., Academic Advisor, SWAC, Brock International, Student Affairs, Learning Services) for early intervention and support to co-op students’ success at Brock;  Identify and provide guidance and support to co-op students facing challenges and barriers, connecting them with appropriate resources and services;   Act as the lead person for all student recruitment events for Co-op (i.e., OUF, Open Houses, Smart Start, etc.); Lead the development of year one (1) co-op student engagement strategy including events, programming, marketing and outreach;   Oversee the planning and execution of our outreach to incoming co-op students, Co-op Kick-off event, program specific onboarding sessions and co-op celebration events for all currently enrolled co-op students;   Oversee and supervise the student staff to carry out communication and marketing plans to enhance co-op students’ engagement including regular newsletter and Co-op-on-the-go booth;   Identify and integrate co-op student success stories and testimonials into communication and marketing collaterals (i.e., websites, social media posts, publications, presentations, etc.); Provide regular report on student retention trends, identify at-risk populations to help measure the effectiveness of the retention initiatives;  Stay current and proficient with various technology platforms such as DB Brock, Sakai, CareerZone and Office 365 suits (i.e., Teams, Planner, Word, Excel, PowerPoint, etc.); and Manage content creation and update to Co-op website and marketing collaterals focusing on students engagement. This position may be required to work occasional evenings and weekends for events or operational needs. Key Skills and Experience: An undergraduate degree in a relevant discipline, such as Psychology, Communication, or Education; Completion of Mental Health and First Aid training; Experience with co-op programs and/or academic advising or student affairs ; Knowledge of the Brock University co-op experience and understanding of the course calendar, course offerings, program requirements, prerequisites, and course sequencing for co-op programs;   Knowledge of applicable University rules and regulations, and departmental policies (i.e. University Academic Accommodations for Students with Accessibility needs Policy);   Proficiency with computers including MS Office Word, Excel, Outlook, Powerpoint databases, web applications, BrockDB, and social media platforms/content creation tools (i.e. Instagram, Linkedin, Canva, etc.); Patience, empathy, compassion, and understanding with the ability to communicate sensitive information or feedback tactfully and diplomatically;  Excellent customer service orientation with the ability to build credibility quickly and maintain long-term relationships;  Proven ability to build and maintain strong professional relationships at all levels of the University and with diverse student populations;  Strong active listening, communication, presentation, and interpersonal skills; Proven ability to organize and handle a variety of projects, deadlines, and schedules simultaneously and independently in a fast-paced environment; and   Innovative, flexible, and adaptable with a commitment to teamwork and integrity.  Preferred or Asset Skills:  A background or experience in counselling or psychology field.   Salary and Total Rewards:  Job Grade K, Salary Scale ($62,115 - $99,384); Target Hiring Range: $62,115-$64,000 * This is a Fixed term contract position that includes:   Health & Dental Benefits: Comprehensive extended health, dental, and vision coverage. Pension Plan: Enrollment eligibility in the Brock University Pension Plan. Vacation: Up to 3 weeks per year, in addition to university holidays. Flexible Work Arrangements: Hybrid work schedule available. *The final salary is based on experience, internal equity, and budget considerations within the target hiring range. We are committed to offering flexible work arrangements where possible as outlined in our Flexible Work Arrangement Policy . As a student-centered organization, all employees are required to work on campus as needed.   Brock University is committed to creating a respectful and equitable workplace. We strive to foster a culture of diversity and inclusion in our work and learning environments. We welcome applications from all qualified individuals and actively encourage applications from women, people with disabilities, members of the 2SLGBTQIA+ community, Indigenous Peoples, people who identify as Black, African and/or Caribbean, as racialized and/or as from ethnic and cultural minority groups, and other underrepresented demographic groups at Brock and in the Niagara region. Brock also recognizes intersectionality and the interconnected identities, histories, and experiences of these aforementioned groups .   We are committed to inclusive and barrier-free recruitment, and we accommodat e the needs of applicants throughout all stages of the recruitment process, as outlined in our Employment Accommodation Policy and consistent with the requirements of the Ontario Human Rights Code . Please contact talent@brocku.ca   if you require a disability related accommodation so we can ensure you r participation needs are met.   Brock University does not use AI Technology at any stage of the recruitment process.   We appreciate all applications received . C andidates that are selected for an interview will be contacted.   Learn more about Brock University by visiting www.brocku.ca ."
